PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The key requirements are equal board control (both Alex/Tyler as sole directors with equal voting shares), Alex paid only via salary (thus his shares must lack dividend rights), Tyler paid via dividends (thus his shares must carry dividend rights), and the ability to admit future investors on a strictly non-voting basis.

Only choice C satisfies the first three points exactly: Alex and Tyler are the sole directors; Alex receives non-dividend Class A voting shares; Tyler receives dividend-bearing Class B voting shares. The corporation is also authorized to issue two additional classes, giving it the formal power to create investor shares if needed. All other options either (a) give both founders identical dividend rights, (b) reverse the dividend allocation, (c) add extra directors who destroy equal control, or (d) authorize only voting shares and therefore cannot admit non-voting investors. C is therefore the only structure that meets every stated criterion.}

